 ## Background
-The standard 3D Gaussian Splatting method [12] uses degree 3 SH coefficients to represent view-dependent color. However, empirical analysis suggests that for many scenes, the higher-order coefficients do not change significantly during training, leading to wasted computation [S1, unofficial]. This observation has led to various optimization strategies, including distillation, quantization, and degree reduction, to accelerate training and reduce memory usage without significant quality loss.
+Spherical Harmonics have long been used in computer graphics for representing lighting and view-dependent effects. In the context of 3D Gaussian Splatting, introduced by Kerbl et al. in 2023, SH coefficients are used to encode the view-dependent color of each Gaussian primitive. The standard implementation uses third-order SH ($d=3$), resulting in 45 coefficients per RGB channel. Subsequent research has identified that this high degree can be optimized for compression and speed without significant quality loss, leading to the development of distillation, quantization, and degree reduction techniques.
 
